    explanation: "SAML assertions are digitally signed by the IdP using its private key. The Service Provider validates this signature using the IdP's public key certificate, which is pre-configured (pinned) in the SP's federation metadata. This provides integrity and non-repudiation — the SP can be confident the assertion was genuinely issued by the trusted IdP and was not tampered with in transit. While TLS protects transport, the assertion signature provides end-to-end trust independent of transport security. SAML does not use shared symmetric keys or secret tokens for assertion trust.",
