    explanation: "Clark-Wilson formalizes the access triple, binding an authenticated subject to a specific transformation procedure and to the constrained data items that procedure may touch, so all changes flow through certified, well-formed transactions rather than direct manipulation. A security lattice is the partially ordered set of labels used by models such as Bell-LaPadula and Biba to determine permitted information flow, not the mediation of access through a program. A reference monitor is the abstract concept requiring that all access between subjects and objects be mediated tamper-proof and verifiably; it describes the enforcement mechanism generally rather than this specific three-part binding. A protection domain is the set of objects and rights available to a process at a point in time, a general operating-system construct rather than a Clark-Wilson element.",
