    answer: 2,
    explanation: "Reporting functionally to the board's audit committee keeps internal audit outside the management chain it examines, so audit results cannot be filtered, softened, or suppressed by the executives whose controls are under review, and it gives auditors the standing to raise uncomfortable findings. Reporting to the chief information security officer would place auditors under the very executive whose security program they must independently evaluate. Reporting to the chief information officer creates the same conflict for information technology controls, since the CIO owns the systems and processes being tested. Reporting to an IT operations manager is even more compromised, placing the audit function beneath a line manager directly responsible for the day-to-day operation of the controls in scope.",
