    answer: 0,
    explanation: "Version control retains the full history of every commit, so a deleted line remains retrievable from prior revisions, from forks and clones already made, and from automated scrapers that monitor public repositories in near real time; the only reliable response is to treat the credential as compromised, revoke and rotate it at the issuing provider, and then purge it from history. Making the repository private afterward does not recall copies already cloned or indexed and leaves the exposed key valid. Editing the commit message changes only metadata and not the stored file contents. Adding the file to an ignore list prevents future accidental commits but does nothing about the value already published.",
