    explanation: "Decommissioning is the final phase of the asset lifecycle, and its correct sequence is to migrate, verify, and only then sanitize and dispose. Verification means confirming record counts, checksums or hashes, metadata, permissions, and the ability of the consuming application to read the content, because migrations routinely drop files, truncate long paths, mangle character encodings, or lose access control metadata in ways that surface only under real use. Sanitizing the source on the same day removes the only fallback before anyone has established that the copy is complete and usable. Retaining the old array permanently as an archive is not appropriate; it multiplies copies of sensitive data on unsupported hardware and conflicts with the retention program, which should govern archival copies on managed media. Skipping sanitization in reliance on the lessor's contractual obligation transfers the task but not the accountability for the data, and the organization cannot verify what a third party did with media it no longer possesses. Waiting a full production year is disproportionate and needlessly costly; a defined verification and stabilization period, not an arbitrary year, is what the plan needs.",
