    explanation: "Forking transfers maintenance responsibility to the forking organization: automated composition analysis will no longer recognize the internal copy as the upstream package, so newly disclosed vulnerabilities in that code will not be flagged automatically, and the team must actively track advisories against the original project and port fixes into its fork. Being internal-only reduces license redistribution obligations but has no bearing on vulnerability exposure, since the vulnerable code still runs in the organization's production systems. Contributing patches upstream is good citizenship and often reduces long-term burden, but it is not a precondition for using a fork internally, particularly when the maintainer is unresponsive. Relicensing someone else's open-source code under a proprietary license is generally not permitted by the original license and is not required for internal use in any case.",
